## 8. Verdict

**Verdict: SPOT-CHECK.** Two of eight headline claims are testable from public data, and only directionally (not quantitatively). One (C4: WT γ-IR upregulates DDR genes) is supported by a 2.05×-fold enrichment + 0.44×-fold depletion against the GSE112773 scaffold. One (C8: AOX1a is a primary WT γ-IR target) is partially contradicted by the same scaffold (AOX1a is in a WT-repressed and SOG1-dependent path). The remaining six claims are wet-lab-only on lab-internal transgenic Arabidopsis lines exposed to a 200 Gy γ-source — none of which is replicable with local free tools. The paper has **no public deposit and no open-access PDF**, so no numerical comparison was possible.

**Scoring.**
- **Coverage = 2/10.** Two of the paper's eight headline claims were testable, and only on the directional axis, in a single genotype (Col-0), at a different dose (100 Gy vs 200 Gy), in a different tissue (seedlings vs 5-wk leaves), at a different time-course resolution. The full biochemistry + phenotype + AS-12 + XX-2 axes were completely off-limits.
- **Agreement = 6/10.** Where I could test, the directional results are mixed-but-mostly-consistent with the paper's qualitative narrative (DDR-induction supported; AOX1a-primary partially contradicted; AOX-family stress induction supported via AOX1c → W7). The discrepancies are real but plausibly explainable by the dose/tissue/timepoint mismatch and by AOX1a's known post-transcriptional regulation.

